{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-831912457869bc28", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\t2026\n2\t\n3\tVoice Memory for Agentic Speech Recognition\n4\tChao-Han Huck Yang*\n5\tBoris Ginsburg\n6\t\n7\tZih-Ching Chen\n8\t\n9\tPiotr Żelasko\n10\t\n11\tZhehuai Chen\n12\t\n13\tJagadeesh Balam\n14\t\n15\tNVIDIA\n16\tCorrespondence: huckiyang@ieee.org\n17\t\n18\t{virginiac, pzelasko, zhehuaic}@nvidia.com\n19\t\n20\t* Work done at NVIDIA.\n21\t\n22\tarXiv:2607.26410v1 [cs.CL] 29 Jul 2026\n23\t\n24\tModel\n25\t\n26\tDemo\n27\t\n28\tNeMo-Speech\n29\t\n30\tNeMoClaw\n31\t\n32\tNotebook\n33\t\n34\tAbstract\n35\tWe present Voice Memory, a inference-only scheme for agentic speech recognition: at stream time, a frozen\n36\tcorrector reads a single per-domain memory.md and decides per utterance whether to act on the hypothesis or\n37\tabstain and keep the 1-best. Asynchronously, a score-gated optimizer revises that file through bounded edits,\n38\taccepting an edit only when it strictly improves a held-out score. Extended from classical ASR-LM framework,\n39\twe refer this split the listener-thinker architecture; the two roles are coupled only through the memory, so no\n40\tweights change and the learned skill stays auditable and portable. Restraint turns out to be the operative\n41\tskill this loop discovers: unconstrained generative error correction (GER) over-corrects, breaking correct\n42\ttokens on up to 64% of its edits on financial news, and Voice Memory reduces this rate to 35%. Across ten\n43\tHyPoradise domains with an open corrector, Voice Memory lowers weighted word error rate from 8.36% to\n44\t7.52% (7.47% with three added in-context examples) without regressing any dataset below its 1-best baseline;\n45\tgains concentrate where recoverable headroom is largest, including air-travel commands (8.40% to 3.40%) and\n46\tnoisy far-field speech (CHiME-4, 12.69% to 10.46%). The memory transfers across corrector families and adds\n47\tzero parameters to the inference path. A demo and example code are provided for future studies.\n48\t\n49\tKeywords: voice agents, memory modeling, on-device, language models, test-time adaptation\n50\t\n51\t1. Introduction\n52\tModern voice systems have entered a low-error regime for clean speech recognition. Decades of progress, from\n53\tstatistical HMM systems [42, 23] to large-scale neural models [3, 13, 10, 43, 40], have brought read speech\n54\tbelow 2% word error rate (WER); yet decoders still make systematic, domain-specific errors [6, 33, 50, 35]\n55\tand preferences [26, 16, 56, 15]. In the classical source-channel formulation of recognition [23], which mediates\n56\tbetween words and memories [9, 38], these are exactly the failures expected when acoustic evidence is reconciled\n57\twith a mismatched language prior. These errors [50, 49] are consistent within a domain but differ across\n58\tdomains: in financial news, an ASR system renders “Bentsen” as “benson”; in air-travel queries, it collapses\n59\tthe spelled form “u s air” into “us air.” A fix tuned for one domain therefore transfers poorly to another.\n60\tA widely adopted remedy is the generative hypothesis-rewriting framework: the 𝑛-best decoding hypotheses\n61\t(i.e., ASR, OCR, or machine translation) are passed to an LLM, which rewrites them into a single transcript\n62\t[6, 44, 53, 61, 12, 60, 5]. For instance, GER [57] extends a long line of language-model rescoring and correction,\n63\tfrom BERT-based hypothesis scoring [47] to edit-aligned neural correction [30] that achieved several state-ofthe-art results in annual speech-translation competitions and on robust ASR benchmarks. In the low-error\n64\tregime, however, a strong LLM tends to over-correct: it rewrites tokens that were already correct, changing\n65\thome is to home’s and normalizing digits and spelling to its own conventions. Each such edit is fluent, yet it\n66\tmoves the output away from the reference transcript; on low-headroom domains, zero-shot GER raises WER\n67\tabove the unedited 1-best (§4). The decision facing a corrector has therefore inverted: at sub-2% WER, the\n68\tquestion is no longer whether the model can fix an error, but whether it should modify the hypothesis at all.\n69\tWe refer to this setting as agentic speech recognition, and we make the term precise. We call an ASR\n70\tsystem agentic when it satisfies three conditions. First, decision: it selects among actions per utterance,\n71\tin our case whether to act on the hypothesis or to abstain and keep it, rather than applying one fixed\n72\ttransformation to every input. Second, persistent state: its decisions are conditioned on an explicit,\n73\t© 2026 NVIDIA. All rights reserved.\n74\t\n75\t\fVoice Memory for Agentic Speech Recognition\n76\t\n77\tUpdating local memory\n78\tcontexts (i.e., CPU)\n79\t\n80\tNo Re-training\n81\t\n82\tTransferable Textualized &\n83\tAcoustic Preferences\n84\t\n85\tDirect inference with\n86\tfixed weights (i.e., GPU)\n87\t\n88\tOn-device\n89\tTraining\n90\t\n91\tFigure 1 | Overview of Voice Memory. At inference, a frozen corrector reads a learnable, human-readable\n92\ttext memory and decides per utterance whether to act on the ASR hypothesis or abstain. Offline, an optimizer\n93\tdistills this instructional memory from training corpora using forward passes only, with no weight updates, so\n94\tthat the learned skill (i) generalizes within an acoustic domain and (ii) transfers across domains and correctors.\n95\tinspectable state that outlives a single utterance, in our case a text memory rather than activations or\n96\tweights. Third, self-improvement: it revises that state from feedback on its own scored outputs, without\n97\thuman intervention. Prior work realizes these properties inside the model, fine-tuning an omni-model to\n98\temit per-utterance action decisions from audio (§2). We study the complementary, test-time realization: the\n99\tdecision policy lives in an external memory that a frozen corrector reads at inference.\n100\tDefinition 1 collects the three conditions into a single format, which we call the listener-thinker (LT)\n101\tarchitecture. It extends the generic ASR-LM cascade, a frozen decoder (𝐵𝜑 ) feeding a frozen language\n102\tmodel 𝑀𝜃 , with exactly the two objects the three conditions require: an explicit action distribution 𝜋 over\n103\t{act, abstain} and a persistent text state (𝑠) that a separate, asynchronous optimizer revises. The listener\n104\tis the synchronous path that decodes and decides at stream time; the thinker is the background path that\n105\timproves 𝑠 between utterances. Table 1 situates the format against the two dominant speech stacks: E2E ASR\n106\tfixes the map and never decides, SpeechLMs always emit and keep their state in weights and context, and\n107\tonly the LT format has slots for evidence (𝐻), external state, a per-utterance action 𝑎, and self-improvement.\n108\tDefinition 1: The Listener-Thinker (LT) Format\n109\tWrite 𝑥 = (𝑥Audio , 𝑥Text ) for audio input and 𝑦^ = (𝑦Text , 𝑦Audio ) for text output. An agentic speech system in the\n110\tlistener-thinker is a tuple (𝐵𝜑 , 𝑀𝜃 , 𝜋, 𝑠, 𝑂𝑝), whose synchronous listener (𝐵𝜑 , 𝑀𝜃 , 𝜋) works with an optimizer (𝑂𝑝):\n111\ta frozen decoder 𝐵𝜑 maps 𝑥 to textual evidence 𝐻 = (ℎ1 , . . . , ℎ𝑛 ); a frozen 𝑀𝜃 , holding\n112\t∑︀ a persistent, inspectable\n113\tstate 𝑠 ∈ Σ* , emits per utterance through an action 𝑎 ∼ 𝜋(· | 𝐻, 𝑠), so that 𝑝(^\n114\t𝑦 | 𝑥, 𝑠) = 𝑎 𝜋(𝑎 | 𝐻, 𝑠) 𝑝𝜃 (^\n115\t𝑦 | 𝐻, 𝑠, 𝑎)\n116\twith:\n117\t{︃\n118\t𝑀𝜃 (𝐻, 𝑠) 𝑎 = act\n119\t𝑦^ =\n120\tℎ1\n121\t𝑎 = abstain,\n122\twhile text state 𝑠 is revised only asynchronously by the score-gated thinker, with 𝜃, 𝜑 fixed. asr-lm (this work)\n123\tand speechlm-lm are the same type at different signatures: 𝑥Text = 𝑦Audio at utterance timescale versus the full\n124\tsignature at frame timescale with 𝒜 ⊇ {act, abstain} (speak, wait, delegate). Constant policies recover the classics:\n125\t𝜋 ≡ abstain is E2E decoding; 𝜋 ≡ act, 𝑠 = ∅ is a zero-shot GER case, when the thinker is with fixed 𝜃 and 𝜑\n126\tparameters with failure rollouts (𝐹 ) and Success rollouts (𝑈 ).\n127\t\n128\t2\n129\t\n130\t\fVoice Memory for Agentic Speech Recognition\n131\t\n132\tTable 1 | Typology of speech systems. The listener-thinker (LT) format subsumes asr-lm (refer to Definition 1,\n133\tutterance timescale) and speechlm-lm (full signature, frame timescale) as one type: only the LT column has\n134\tslots for evidence 𝐻, external text state 𝑠, a per-utterance action 𝑎, and test-time self-improvement.\n135\tE2E ASR\n136\t\n137\tSpeechLM\n138\t\n139\tListener-Thinker (agentic; cascaded)\n140\t\n141\t𝑝𝜑 (𝑦Text | 𝑥Audio )\n142\t\n143\t𝑝𝜃 (𝑦Text , 𝑦Audio | 𝑥Text , 𝑥Audio )\n144\t\n145\t𝜋(𝑎 | 𝐻, 𝑠) 𝑝𝜃 (^\n146\t𝑦 | 𝐻, 𝑠, 𝑎)\n147\t\n148\t𝑥Audio\n149\t\n150\t𝑥Audio , 𝑥Text\n151\t\n152\t𝐻 ∼ 𝐵𝜑 (𝑥)\n153\t\n154\tweights 𝜑\n155\t\n156\tweights + contexts.\n157\t\n158\texternal text 𝑠 ∈ Σ*\n159\t\n160\tDecision\n161\t\n162\tnone (fixed map)\n163\t\n164\tnone (always emit)\n165\t\n166\t𝒜 ⊇ {act, abstain}\n167\t\n168\tUpdate\n169\t\n170\toffline ∇𝜑\n171\t\n172\toffline ∇𝜃 / ICL\n173\t\n174\t𝑠′ = 𝑂𝑝(𝑠, 𝐹, 𝑈 ), 𝑣 ↑ async\n175\t\n176\tObjective\n177\t\n178\tWER\n179\t\n180\tlikelihood\n181\t\n182\tverifiable rewards vs. oracle\n183\t\n184\tInstances\n185\t\n186\tWhisper [43], Parakeet [46]\n187\t\n188\tMoshi [7], SALMONN [51]\n189\t\n190\tASR-LMs [57], DuplexLM-LMs [27]\n191\t\n192\tDistribution\n193\tEvidence\n194\tState\n195\t\n196\tRemark 1 (Relation to interaction models). The LT format is the speech instance of the interaction/background split now emerging at the frontier [52, 20]: the listener is the synchronous interaction path,\n197\tthe thinker is the asynchronous background path, and the two are coupled only through the persistent artifact\n198\ttext state, 𝑠. The artifact is what makes the pair auditable and portable.\n199\tExpressing such a policy with existing adaptation mechanisms is costly. Fine-tuning, LoRA [14], and soft\n200\tprompts [31] place the adaptation in weights or continuous vectors, which cannot be inspected, transferred\n201\tacross model families, or revised without retraining; few-shot exemplars [4] recur a token cost on every request.\n202\tNone yields an adaptation that is simultaneously inference-only, auditable, and portable across correctors.\n203\tWe present Voice Memory, which stores the correction skill in a text file. The corrector stays frozen\n204\tand reads a single human-readable memory.md at test time (Figure 1). A separate optimizer converts scored\n205\trollouts into bounded add/delete/replace edits and accepts an edit only when it strictly improves a held-out\n206\tscore [59]. The dominant policy this loop discovers is restraint: from its own scored mistakes, the optimizer\n207\twrites suppressive rules such as “keep the verbatim ASR token; do not normalize,” with no human in\n208\tthe loop. Where recoverable headroom exists, Voice Memory closes most of the gap between the 1-best and\n209\tthe oracle; a second corrector running the same loop even surpasses the 𝑛-best oracle by recovering tokens\n210\tpresent in no hypothesis. We will later introduce new measurement metrics of (i) recoverable information\n211\tratio (𝜌) and (ii) harmful edit rate on these two scenarios for agentic speech recognition.\n212\tThis paper makes four contributions:\n213\t1. Method. Voice Memory, an inference-only adaptation scheme for agentic speech recognition: the\n214\tcorrection skill resides in a per-domain text memory that a validation-gated loop improves through bounded\n215\tedits, with no weight updates (§3).\n216\t2. Formulation and diagnostics. A formal account of the act/abstain decision as the listener-thinker\n217\tformat (Definition 1, Table 1), together with two measures: the Recoverable Information Ratio (𝜌) for\n218\trecovery against the 𝑛-best oracle, and the Harmful Edit Rate (her, Figure 2) for over-correction. Across\n219\tnine domains, the gain from correction tracks recoverable headroom (𝑟=+0.90), delineating the operating\n220\tregime in which a corrector should act (§4).\n221\t3. Portability. Evidence that the optimized memory is a transferable, corrector-agnostic artifact: a memory\n222\twritten by one model family improves a reader from another, and that reader can re-form its own with the\n223\tsame loop (§5).\n224\t4. Noise robustness. The same restraint policy holds under acoustic noise on CHiME-4 and NOIZEUS,\n225\tmatching training-based robust correction without fine-tuning or latent noise embeddings (§6).\n226\tTaken together, Voice Memory offers a low-cost adaptation path for cascades of ASR and LLM correction:\n227\tno training infrastructure, a sub-10 KB auditable artifact, and a measured account of when a corrector should\n228\tact and when it should hold still.\n229\t\n230\t3\n231\t\n232\t\fVoice Memory for Agentic Speech Recognition\n233\t\n234\tTable 2 | Adapting a cascade of ASR and LLM correction: weight space adaptation fine-tuning FT) versus\n235\tVoice Memory. Voice Memory runs forward passes only on the same frozen endpoint used at deployment,\n236\tso it needs no training infrastructure and stays energy efficient on device. Figures are order-of-magnitude.\n237\tFT / LoRA\n238\t\n239\tVoice Memory\n240\t\n241\tforward + backward\n242\t\n243\tforward only\n244\t\n245\tWall-clock\n246\t\n247\thours+\n248\t\n249\tminutes+\n250\t\n251\tAdapt data\n252\t\n253\t103 to 104 utts\n254\t\n255\t∼102 utts\n256\t\n257\tModel Params.\n258\t\n259\t106 to 109\n260\t\n261\t0\n262\t\n263\tArtifact size\n264\t\n265\tGB / MB\n266\t\n267\t<10 KB\n268\t\n269\tUser Auditable\n270\t\n271\t✗\n272\t\n273\t✓\n274\t\n275\tPortable\n276\t\n277\t✗\n278\t\n279\t✓\n280\t\n281\tReversible\n282\t\n283\t✗\n284\t\n285\t✓\n286\t\n287\tAdaptation pass\n288\t\n289\t2. Related Work\n290\tASR-LM and Agentic Setup for ASR. Recent work treats audio LLMs as agents that decide how to use\n291\tinternal perception and external evidence, rather than decoding passively [54, 34]. Speech-Hands [54] and\n292\tSpeech-Mind [55] are the closest to our work. These setups learn a per-utterance action token by fine-tuning\n293\tan omni-model on speech and audio reasoning, so its agency is acoustic, learned in weights, and re-decided\n294\teach utterance. Voice Memory differs on each of these axes (§1): it learns one accumulated memory, uses\n295\tno audio and no training, and runs entirely at inference. The two methods are the trained and test-time\n296\tversions of one idea, an ASR system that decides when to trust its own output.\n297\tGenerative error correction and contextual biasing. Injecting domain-specific knowledge into ASR\n298\toutput is a long-standing goal. Early systems brought contextual 𝑛-grams into decoding through shallow\n299\tfusion and on-the-fly rescoring [2, 63]. End-to-end systems then moved biasing into the model: CLAS [39]\n300\tjointly embeds bias phrases and attends to them during decoding, and later work extended this to RNN-T\n301\t[22] and to trie-based deep biasing [29]. These methods adapt the acoustic model and require a bias list and a\n302\ttraining run. LLM-based GER instead recasts correction as a language task over 𝑛-best hypotheses [6, 57, 44],\n303\tadapting the corrector through fine-tuning or in-context exemplars. Voice Memory sits at the opposite end\n304\tof both lines: it leaves the corrector frozen and writes the domain’s conventions into a text memory read at\n305\tinference, with no bias list, no training, and no weight updates.\n306\tNoise-robust ASR and robust hypotheses. One popular ASR-LM use case is Robust-ASR. Acoustic\n307\tnoise makes correction harder because the 𝑛-best list itself degrades, so a corrector must denoise rather than\n308\trerank. The Robust HyPoradise benchmark [18] pairs noisy Whisper hypotheses with clean references across\n309\tCHiME-4, VoiceBank-DEMAND, LibriSpeech-FreeSound, and NOIZEUS. RobustGER distills the diversity\n310\tof those hypotheses into a language-space noise embedding and fine-tunes the corrector against it, which\n311\trecovers much of the lost accuracy. Cross-modality correctors such as NeKo [34] extend this with task-guided\n312\tmixtures of experts. Both methods read the noise condition in a latent vector and pay for it with a training\n313\trun. Voice Memory uses the same observation, that the 𝑛-best spread already encodes the noise condition,\n314\tbut writes the prior into a per-noise-family text memory that the frozen corrector reads at inference (§6).\n315\tText-space optimization. Optimizing natural-language artifacts such as prompts and skills against a score\n316\tis an alternative to gradient updates [62, 58, 59]. We apply this to ASR correction, where the 𝑛-best oracle\n317\tsets a bounded target (𝜌) and the main learned behavior is restraint.\n318\tWER critique and semantics. Prior work questions WER as the only ASR metric [50, 25]. We add two\n319\tresults. We measure the gap between surface error and meaning (§7.2), and we show that a semantic training\n320\tsignal gives lower WER than a traditional WER signal (§7.1).\n321\t\n322\t4\n323\t\n324\t\fVoice Memory for Agentic Speech Recognition\n325\t\n326\t3. The Voice Memory Method\n327\tThe corrector never changes; only its memory does. We formalize correction as a per-utterance decision to\n328\tact or abstain (§3.1), define the object we optimize and the inference-only loop that optimizes it (§3.2), and\n329\tdefine a measure of over-correction used in our analysis (§3.3).\n330\t3.1. Problem: correction against an oracle ceiling\n331\tLet a domain provide 𝑛-best hypotheses 𝐻 = (ℎ1 , . . . , ℎ𝑛 ) from a frozen acoustic model, with ℎ1 the 1-best.\n332\tA corrector produces a single transcript from 𝐻. At low error rates the corrector’s main decision is not which\n333\thypothesis to pick but whether to change ℎ1 at all, so we treat correction as a per-utterance choice to act or\n334\tabstain.\n335\tThe 𝑛-best list bounds what reranking can achieve. The oracle selects, per utterance, the hypothesis in\n336\t𝐻 with the lowest WER against the reference, giving WERoracle . Reranking cannot go below this bound;\n337\tcorrection can, because it may produce tokens that appear in no hypothesis. We measure how much of the\n338\t1-best-to-oracle gap a correction closes with the Recoverable Information Ratio (𝜌):\n339\tWER1-best − WER𝑦^\n340\t,\n341\t(1)\n342\tWER1-best − WERoracle\n343\twhere 𝑦^ is the corrected transcript. 𝜌=1 closes the gap exactly. 𝜌<0 is the damage regime, where correcting\n344\tis worse than keeping ℎ1 . 𝜌>1 means the corrector recovers tokens present in no hypothesis and goes below\n345\tthe oracle bound. 𝜌 is the target the method aims at, and §4 reports it per domain.\n346\t𝜌=\n347\t\n348\tTo analyze why a correction helps, we also label each token edit as helpful (fixed a wrong token) or harmful\n349\t(broke a correct one); the Harmful Edit Rate (her) is the harmful fraction, and isolates over-correction. We\n350\treport her on a subset of domains (Figure 2).\n351\t3.2. Voice Memory and the optimization loop\n352\tA frozen corrector 𝑀 reads 𝐻 and an optional memory 𝑠, and emits 𝑦^ = 𝑀 (𝐻, 𝑠). The memory 𝑠 is a short\n353\tMarkdown document, placed in the corrector’s context before each utterance. With 𝑠 = ∅ this reduces to\n354\tstandard GER. We keep 𝑠 in text, external to 𝑀 , so that it can be read and edited by an operator, reused\n355\tacross correctors, and added with no change to the inference path. A learned 𝑠 is a small set of rules, for\n356\texample:\n357\tkeep the verbatim ASR token; do not normalize.\n358\tplace ‘dollars’ after the amount, not ‘$’ before it.\n359\tWe optimize 𝑠 on a domain’s training split with a trajectory feedback loop [1, 37, 59], treating 𝑠 as the\n360\texternal state of the frozen 𝑀 .\n361\tThe three splits stay disjoint: train supplies rollout evidence, selection gates acceptance, and test is used\n362\tonce, for reporting. At deployment the frozen 𝑀 reads the best accepted 𝑠. No weights change, and no extra\n363\tmodel calls occur. The rules the loop accepts are mostly suppressive; §4 shows the learned policy is restraint.\n364\t3.3. Measuring over-correction\n365\tWER reports whether a correction helped, not why. We add the Harmful Edit Rate (her). We align 𝑦^\n366\tagainst ℎ1 and the reference, then label each token edit as helpful (fixed a wrong token), harmful (broke a\n367\tcorrect token), or missed. her is the fraction of all token edits that are harmful. It isolates over-correction: a\n368\tcorrector that edits tokens that were already correct has a high her.\n369\tWe evaluate on HyPoradise v0 [6], which pairs Whisper 5-best hypotheses with references across 10 domains\n370\tunder disjoint train/test splits: clean read speech (ls_clean), financial news (wsj), voice commands (atis),\n371\ttelephone and meeting speech (swbd, chime4), and accented or conversational speech (cv, coraal, lrs2, td3,\n372\tls_other). A single frozen instruction LLM, the open-source MiniMax-M3 [28] with 1M context and ∼428B\n373\tparameters, serves as both corrector and optimizer.1 Semantic scoring, used to gate some memories and\n374\t1We use MiniMax-M3; the model checkpoint has fixed, open weights: https://huggingface.co/MiniMaxAI/MiniMax-M3.\n375\t\n376\t5\n377\t\n378\t\fVoice Memory for Agentic Speech Recognition\n379\t\n380\tAlgorithm 1: Voice Memory optimization. All model calls are forward passes; no gradients, no\n381\tweight updates.\n382\tInput: frozen 𝑀 , optimizer 𝑂𝑝; splits 𝐷train , 𝐷sel ; epochs 𝐸; budget {ℓ𝑒 }\n383\tOutput: memory 𝑠⋆\n384\t𝑠, 𝑠⋆ ← ∅;\n385\t𝑅 ← ∅;\n386\t𝑣 ⋆ ← Score(𝑀, ∅, 𝐷sel )\n387\tfor 𝑒 ← 1 to 𝐸 do\n388\tforeach batch 𝐻 ⊂ 𝐷train do\n389\t𝑌^ ← {𝑀 (ℎ, 𝑠) : ℎ ∈ 𝐻}; 𝐹, 𝑈 ← failures, successes\n390\t𝑠′ ← 𝑂𝑝(𝑠, 𝐹, 𝑈, 𝑅, ℓ𝑒 ); 𝑣 ′ ← Score(𝑀, 𝑠′ , 𝐷sel )\n391\tif 𝑣 ′ > 𝑣 ⋆ then\n392\t𝑠⋆ ← 𝑠 ← 𝑠 ′ ;\n393\t𝑣⋆ ← 𝑣′\n394\telse\n395\t𝑅 ← 𝑅 ∪ {𝑠′ }\n396\t\n397\t// rollout\n398\t// ≤ ℓ𝑒 edits\n399\t\n400\t// accept\n401\t// reject\n402\t\n403\treturn 𝑠⋆\n404\t\n405\tTable 3 | Voice Memory (semantic-gated) across HyPoradise full test splits, same corrector (MiniMax-M3428B ).\n406\tBold marks the lowest WER among GER0-shot , GER5-shot , and VM per row. The gain follows the scaling\n407\tproperties: 𝜌VM is high on high-WER atis/cv and negative at the near-floor td3/ls_clean.\n408\tDomain\n409\t\n410\t1-best\n411\t\n412\tOracle\n413\t\n414\tger0\n415\t\n416\tger5\n417\t\n418\tVMsem\n419\t\n420\t𝜌VM\n421\t\n422\tatis\n423\twsj\n424\tchime4\n425\tcv\n426\tlrs2\n427\tcoraal\n428\ttd3\n429\tls_o\n430\tls_c\n431\t\n432\t7.9\n433\t4.9\n434\t9.9\n435\t15.4\n436\t11.7\n437\t25.6\n438\t4.1\n439\t3.7\n440\t1.8\n441\t\n442\t4.7\n443\t3.6\n444\t7.5\n445\t11.3\n446\t6.7\n447\t23.7\n448\t2.9\n449\t2.3\n450\t0.8\n451\t\n452\t6.3\n453\t5.8\n454\t9.7\n455\t13.1\n456\t11.1\n457\t25.1\n458\t4.4\n459\t4.1\n460\t2.4\n461\t\n462\t5.2\n463\t4.7\n464\t9.4\n465\t12.7\n466\t10.5\n467\t25.9\n468\t4.3\n469\t4.0\n470\t2.5\n471\t\n472\t4.9\n473\t4.3\n474\t9.4\n475\t13.0\n476\t10.9\n477\t25.3\n478\t4.2\n479\t3.6\n480\t1.9\n481\t\n482\t0.96\n483\t0.48\n484\t0.21\n485\t0.59\n486\t0.16\n487\t0.15\n488\t−0.09\n489\t0.06\n490\t−0.11\n491\t\n492\tanalyzed in §7, runs on an on-device sentence encoder (all-MiniLM-L6-v2, 4-bit; [45]) and adds no API cost.\n493\tWe report the full agwer python suite, with WER shown alongside 𝜌 and her.2\n494\t\n495\t4. Over-Correction and Scaling Properties\n496\tOver-correction and restraint. The core failure of unconstrained correction is over-correction: zero-shot\n497\tGER edits tokens that were already correct. In Table 3 it raises WER above the 1-best on the low-headroom\n498\tdomains (ls_clean 1.8 → 2.4, ls_other 3.7 → 4.1, td3 4.1 → 4.4), and on four labeled domains it is harmful\n499\ton a large fraction of its edits, up to 0.64 on wsj (Figure 2). Voice Memory outperforms GER on four\n500\ttasks. Moreover, it lowers the harmful-edit rate on every labeled domain (to 0.35 on wsj; Figure 2), and the\n501\tdeployed wsj memory is 776 bytes.\n502\tThe gain scales with WER. One benefit of correction is that it scales with recoverable headroom, measured\n503\tby 𝜌: largest where 1-best WER is high, and vanishing at the error floor. Across the nine domains of Table 3,\n504\t𝜌 correlates with 1-best WER at 𝑟=+0.90. Voice Memory helps most where headroom is largest: at the\n505\tls_clean floor (𝜌=−0.11) and td3 (𝜌=−0.09) little remains to recover, while on high-WER atis it recovers\n506\tmost of the gap (𝜌=0.96). Few-shot prompting is competitive on some domains but pays a token cost per call,\n507\twhereas the Voice Memory memory is formed once and read for free.\n508\t2WER and her are computed with our open-source agwer package (https://pypi.org/project/agwer/ under MIT license),\n509\tan efficient implementation that is safe to use as a reward target inside the optimization loop.\n510\t\n511\t6\n512\t\n513\t\fHarmful Edit Rate (HER)\n514\t\n515\tVoice Memory for Agentic Speech Recognition\n516\t\n517\t0.8\n518\t\n519\tStatic GER\n520\tVoice Memory\n521\t\n522\t0.74\n523\t\n524\t0.64\n525\t\n526\t0.62\n527\t\n528\t0.6\n529\t\n530\t0.59\n531\t\n532\t0.43\n533\t\n534\t0.4\n535\t\n536\t0.35\n537\t0.25\n538\t\n539\t0.2\n540\t0.10\n541\t\n542\t0.0\n543\t\n544\tatis\n545\t\n546\twsj\n547\t\n548\tls_other\n549\t\n550\tls_clean\n551\t\n552\tFigure 2 | Harmful Edit Rate (HER) on four HyPoradise domains. Static GER over-corrects; Voice Memory\n553\tlowers HER on every domain. Lower is better.\n554\tTable 4 | Robust Voice Memory (VM) on the Robust HyPoradise corpora [18]. Voice Memory lowers\n555\tWER on the recorded-noise corpora (CHiME-4, NOIZEUS), where static GER over-corrects, and is near-null\n556\ton read speech with added noise (VoiceBank-DEMAND, LS-FreeSound; discussed in the text). 𝜌vm is the\n557\trecovered fraction of the 1-best-to-oracle gap; bold marks the best WER per row.\n558\tCondition\n559\t\n560\t1-best Oracle Static\n561\t\n562\tVM\n563\t\n564\t𝜌vm\n565\t\n566\tCHiME-4 (real recorded / simulated noise)\n567\ttest-real\n568\t9.8\n569\t7.8\n570\t9.9 9.5\n571\t0.17\n572\ttest-simu\n573\t12.7\n574\t10.3\n575\t12.5 11.7 0.39\n576\tdev-real\n577\t7.8\n578\t6.3\n579\t8.1 7.3\n580\t0.31\n581\tdev-simu\n582\t9.7\n583\t7.9\n584\t9.8 8.8 0.47\n585\tNOIZEUS, pooled by SNR\n586\t0 dB\n587\t42.9\n588\t34.9\n589\t5 dB\n590\t14.5\n591\t9.2\n592\t10 dB\n593\t5.0\n594\t2.1\n595\t15 dB\n596\t2.7\n597\t0.9\n598\t\n599\t41.5 41.4\n600\t0.19\n601\t14.8 12.6 0.36\n602\t5.2 4.9\n603\t0.05\n604\t2.7\n605\t2.8 −0.06\n606\t\n607\t5. Memory Transfer and Portability\n608\tSince the memory is in text form, it is transferable to different models. We show two additional studies: a\n609\tmemory written by one corrector helps a second corrector from a different family, and that second corrector\n610\tcan form its own memory with the same loop.\n611\tA memory transfers to a corrector from a different family. We test a second frozen corrector,\n612\tAnthropic Claude-4.6-Sonnet (2026 February), at inference. On atis, Claude reads a MiniMax-written\n613\tmemory and improves over its no-memory baseline (6.68% to 6.08% WER, Table 5). The reader improves\n614\twith a memory it did not write, so the memory is a separate component, not a property of the writer.\n615\tA corrector can form its own memory. Running the same validation-gated loop with Claude as both\n616\tcorrector and optimizer gives a self-formed memory that reaches 𝜌=1.28 on atis, above the MiniMax memory\n617\tread by Claude and above the 𝑛-best oracle (3.94% vs. 6.08% WER, Table 5).\n618\tTransfer follows the scaling properties. The transfer gain concentrates where there is recoverable\n619\theadroom, as §4 predicts. Across the four domains of Table 5, a memory helps on high-WER atis but is\n620\tneutral to slightly harmful at the clean floor (ls_clean, ls_other), where little headroom remains. The\n621\tlearned rules are general restraint rather than a domain-specific fix, so the memory is reusable and can be\n622\tapplied to different models.\n623\t\n624\t7\n625\t\n626\t\fVoice Memory for Agentic Speech Recognition\n627\t\n628\tTable 5 | A second frozen corrector (Claude-4.6-Sonnet) at inference on a 100-utterance test, varying only the\n629\tmemory. “+MiniMax” reads the memory optimized in §4; “+Claude” reads a memory Claude formed itself\n630\twith the same validation-gated loop (§3.2). On atis the self-formed memory beats the MiniMax one and the\n631\t𝑛-best oracle (𝜌=1.28); on data-hungry wsj it over-corrects; at the low-WER floor no memory is best.\n632\tDomain\n633\t\n634\t1-best\n635\t\n636\toracle\n637\t\n638\tno mem\n639\t\n640\t+ mmax\n641\t\n642\t+ claude\n643\t\n644\tatis\n645\twsj\n646\tls_other\n647\tls_clean\n648\t\n649\t8.2\n650\t5.3\n651\t4.8\n652\t1.9\n653\t\n654\t4.9\n655\t4.1\n656\t3.3\n657\t0.7\n658\t\n659\t6.7\n660\t6.1\n661\t4.3\n662\t1.7\n663\t\n664\t6.1\n665\t4.6\n666\t4.5\n667\t1.8\n668\t\n669\t3.9\n670\t6.2\n671\t4.8\n672\t1.8\n673\t\n674\tTable 6 | Voice Memory for X→En translation correction (BLEU ↑). Voice Memory improves over the 1-best\n675\tin every direction (𝜌>0); shown are the three directions where the formed memory also beats memory-free\n676\treconciliation (fr, where reconciliation alone is strongest, is discussed in Appendix D). 𝜌 is recoverable BLEU,\n677\t(vm − 1-best)/(oracle − 1-best).\n678\tLang\n679\t\n680\t1-best\n681\t\n682\tOracle\n683\t\n684\tzero-shot\n685\t\n686\tvm\n687\t\n688\t𝜌vm\n689\t\n690\tde\n691\tja\n692\tzh\n693\t\n694\t33.4\n695\t20.5\n696\t25.3\n697\t\n698\t42.9\n699\t35.2\n700\t28.9\n701\t\n702\t34.4\n703\t24.1\n704\t25.6\n705\t\n706\t34.6\n707\t24.5\n708\t26.5\n709\t\n710\t0.13\n711\t0.27\n712\t0.32\n713\t\n714\tThe mechanism generalizes beyond ASR. The recipe extends beyond speech recognition to speechtranslation error correction (the GenTranslate setting [19]), in which a corrector reconciles the 𝑛-best\n715\tEnglish-translation hypotheses of a non-English utterance and is scored by BLEU [32]. In Table 6, we run\n716\tthe identical Voice Memory loop across four X→En directions. Voice Memory improves BLEU over the\n717\t1-best in every direction, recovering 13 to 32% of the oracle headroom, and the optimizer learns the same\n718\trestraint it learns for ASR: it declines to encode reference content present in no hypothesis, and rejects edits\n719\tthat would raise validation BLEU only by copying a single reference. A frozen corrector improving by editing\n720\ta text memory is thus a general recipe for 𝑛-best language correction, not an ASR trick. This is a 40-utterance\n721\tmini-study with a single corrector; the full setup is in Appendix D.\n722\tMemory provenance: quality beats coverage. Because the voice memory file is editable, its source\n723\tmatters more than its size. A memory generated by Claude Sonnet 4.6 reaches the best weighted WER\n724\tat 7.17%, ahead of the auto-generated memory (7.52), the expert hand-crafted memory (7.43), and the\n725\texpert-seeded memory (7.32). The Claude memory is not the largest: at 5.4 KB it is smaller than the 9.0 KB\n726\tauto-generated and 7.6 KB expert-seeded variants. Precise substitution entries and specific formatting rules\n727\tgovern correction quality more than raw coverage.\n728\t\n729\t6. Robust Voice Memory for Noisy ASR\n730\tThe restraint policy of §4 also holds under acoustic noise. RobustGER [18] makes a corrector noise-robust\n731\tby distilling the 𝑛-best diversity into a language-space noise embedding and fine-tuning LLaMA against\n732\tit, which costs one training run per deployment. Voice Memory writes the same prior into a text file.\n733\tWe run the same pipeline on Whisper 𝑛-best from four noise families (CHiME-4, VoiceBank-DEMAND,\n734\tLibriSpeech+FreeSound, NOIZEUS), optimize one semantic-gated memory per family, and read it at inference\n735\twith no weight updates. Our VoiceBank-DEMAND 1-best WERs match RobustGER Table 1 to within 0.1\n736\tpoint (party 22.59 vs. 22.6, baby 7.98 vs. 8.0), so the two setups are comparable.\n737\tThe gain tracks how hard the speech is. As the scaling properties of §4 predict, Voice Memory helps\n738\twhere 1-best WER and oracle headroom are large, and does nothing where they are small (Table 4). It\n739\tlowers WER on the genuinely degraded corpora, CHiME-4 and NOIZEUS, where static GER over-corrects.\n740\tOn CHiME-4 dev-real, static GER raises WER (7.75 → 8.13) while Voice Memory lowers it (7.75 → 7.31,\n741\t𝜌=0.31); on NOIZEUS [17] at 5 dB, Voice Memory recovers 14.51 → 12.60 (𝜌=0.36; both in Table 4). On\n742\tVoiceBank-DEMAND and LS-FreeSound, which are read speech with noise added rather than recorded,\n743\t\n744\t8\n745\t\n746\t\fVoice Memory for Agentic Speech Recognition\n747\t\n748\tTable 7 | Gate ablation, full test, two seeds. Optimizing the memory for semantic preservation beats optimizing\n749\tit for WER on WER, and is ∼4× more seed-stable on ls_clean (range 0.03 vs. 0.17).\n750\tDomain\n751\t\n752\tGate\n753\t\n754\tWER% (mean)\n755\t\n756\tWER range\n757\t\n758\twsj\n759\t\n760\tWER\n761\tSemantic\n762\t\n763\t4.35\n764\t4.16\n765\t\n766\t[4.17, 4.54]\n767\t[4.08, 4.23]\n768\t\n769\tls_clean\n770\t\n771\tWER\n772\tSemantic\n773\t\n774\t2.16\n775\t2.12\n776\t\n777\t[2.08, 2.25]\n778\t[2.10, 2.13]\n779\t\n780\trestraint is already the right policy and the memory is near-null (party 𝜌=0.03). The result shows that\n781\tthe same restraint policy holds under noise: Voice Memory matches RobustGER-style robustness on the\n782\tdegraded corpora with no fine-tuning and no latent noise vector, and correctly abstains where there is nothing\n783\tto recover.\n784\t\n785\t7. Analysis: Meaning versus Surface Error\n786\tWe consider that two questions sit underneath the scaling properties of §4. First, which signal should the\n787\toptimizer gate on: the metric we report (i.e., WER and HER), or the quantity we actually care about\n788\t(meaning)? Second, why would a meaning-based signal help at all? We answer both with measurements on the\n789\tsame frozen corrector, and both answers reinforce the central finding that good correction is mostly restraint.\n790\t7.1. Meaning Is a Better Training Signal than WER\n791\tThe validation gate in §3.2 can score candidates by any metric. We compare two: a WER gate (−WER on\n792\tthe selection split) and a semantic gate (mean cosine similarity between corrected and reference embeddings\n793\tfrom the on-device encoder). The two gates see the same rollouts, the same candidate edits, and the same\n794\tacceptance rule; they differ only in the scalar they maximize, which isolates the effect of the objective itself.\n795\tWe optimize both over two seeds and report on the full test split (Table 7).\n796\tThe semantic gate wins on WER across both domains and both seeds, and resists the optimization seed\n797\tfar better. The mechanism follows from §4: the optimal correction policy is mostly restraint, and a semantic\n798\tobjective rewards restraint without chasing the orthographic ghosts (homophones, spelling variants) that a\n799\tWER objective is tempted to “fix.” A WER gate, by construction, credits any edit that removes a surface\n800\tmismatch. This includes the meaning-preserving ones a careful reader would leave alone; the semantic gate\n801\twithholds that credit, so fewer such edits survive acceptance. Optimizing the metric you report (i.e., WER) is\n802\tthus worse than optimizing the quantity you care about (meaning), which WER only imperfectly tracks. We\n803\tframe this honestly: the margin is modest (∼0.2 points), yet consistent in direction across domains and seeds,\n804\tand the stability and meaning-preservation gains are unambiguous.\n805\t7.2. Language Relativity: a Measurable Surface to Meaning Gap\n806\tWe provide additional study inspired by Hoijer [11] on Whorf–Sapir hypothesis. A semantic objective can beat\n807\tWER on WER because the two quantities live on different axes, and we make this separation quantitative.\n808\tFor each utterance we measure a surface distance and a semantic distance (cos of sentence embeddings).\n809\tRegressing semantic on surface distance yields a decoupling slope; the fraction of surface-error mass whose\n810\tsemantic distance falls below a small threshold is the benign mass (Table 8).\n811\tThe slope sits well below 1 in every domain: surface errors move meaning only weakly. Most residual\n812\terror mass is semantically benign. This measured by an independent on-device model, not asserted. This\n813\tis an operational, falsifiable statement of linguistic relativity for ASR: the text string and the underlying\n814\tmessage are distinct, and WER conflates them. It explains why over-correction is so costly: the corrector\n815\ttrades meaning-neutral surface forms for a lower apparent error count while leaving the message unchanged.\n816\tIt also explains why a semantic gate generalizes better. The practical consequence is a ceiling on how much\n817\tresidual WER is worth pursuing: once an edit moves only the surface form and not the meaning, chasing it\n818\ttrades risk of damage for no communicative gain.\n819\t9\n820\t\n821\t\fVoice Memory for Agentic Speech Recognition\n822\t\n823\tTable 8 | A measurable surface to meaning gap. The decoupling slope (0.38 to 0.60, ≪ 1) means a unit of\n824\tWER moves meaning by only ∼half a unit; 53 to 68% of residual error mass preserves meaning (semantic\n825\tdistance < 0.15).\n826\tDomain\n827\t\n828\tWER\n829\t(surf.)\n830\t\n831\tDecoupling\n832\tslope\n833\t\n834\tBenign\n835\tmass\n836\t\n837\tatis\n838\twsj\n839\tls_other\n840\tls_clean\n841\t\n842\t.080\n843\t.046\n844\t.046\n845\t.021\n846\t\n847\t0.60\n848\t0.38\n849\t0.56\n850\t0.59\n851\t\n852\t66.4%\n853\t67.5%\n854\t53.4%\n855\t65.6%\n856\t\n857\t7.3. Error Analysis\n858\tThe residual after Voice Memory splits into two buckets: genuine acoustic confusions (named entities, rare\n859\twords) that no language prior can resolve, and annotation artifacts (spelling variants, spacing) that should not\n860\tbe “corrected.” The first bucket is a limit of the cascade rather than of the memory. The information needed\n861\tto recover the token is simply absent from the 𝑛-best list. The second is exactly where an unconstrained\n862\tcorrector does damage, and where the learned restraint pays off. The her/𝜌 decomposition (§3.3) separates\n863\tthese, and the benign-mass measure (§7.2) bounds how much of the remaining WER is worth chasing at all.\n864\tAppendix E gives eight representative such cases.\n865\t\n866\t8. Conclusion\n867\tVoice Memory makes a frozen corrector better without touching a single weight. Everything the system\n868\tlearns lives in a small, auditable text memory read at inference time; the only difference between a worse\n869\tcorrector and a better one is that file. A better agent here is simply a better text file. The policy the file\n870\tcomes to encode is restraint, knowing which tokens to leave alone, and this is why we cast correction as a\n871\tper-utterance decision to act or abstain rather than as a sequence-to-sequence rewrite, with a listener that\n872\tdecides at stream time and a thinker that revises the memory asynchronously.\n873\tThe pieces compose into one picture. The gain scales with recoverable headroom: the same policy that\n874\trepairs far-field speech holds still on clean read speech. A memory written by one model family improves a\n875\treader from another, because explicit rules travel where weights cannot; the same trust can be trained into\n876\tweights from audio [54], but Voice Memory acquires it from inference-time feedback, with backpropagation.\n877\tThe cheapest adaptation and personalization we know of for an ASR-LLM cascade. We would refocus the task\n878\titself: optimize not orthography but meaning, and decide not how often to act but when to hold still [36, 48].\n879\t\n880\tLimitations\n881\tOur main analyses use a single open-source model with 428B parameters and 23B activated parameters,\n882\tMiniMax-M3. Appendix B reproduces the core effect with an open Qwen3-30B-A3B corrector across ten\n883\tdatasets. 𝜌 and the benign-mass measure depend on the sentence encoder we use. We reduce this dependence\n884\twith a small, public, on-device model with an average system latency of 610 ms, and by releasing the protocol\n885\tand audio-visual memory formation [24, 8].\n886\t\n887\tEthics Statement\n888\tVoice Memory produces a human-readable artifact, which makes auditing and bias review easier than\n889\tinspecting weight updates. Domain memories can encode dataset-specific conventions, such as spelling norms,\n890\tthat should not transfer across dialects or populations without review. The benign-mass analysis is one way\n891\tto surface these conventions before deployment.\n892\t\n893\t10\n894\t\n895\t\fVoice Memory for Agentic Speech Recognition\n896\t\n897\tReferences\n898\t[1] Lakshya A Agrawal, Shangyin Tan, Dilara Soylu, Noah Ziems, Rishi Khare, Krista Opsahl-Ong, Arnav\n899\tSinghvi, Herumb Shandilya, Michael J Ryan, Meng Jiang, et al. Gepa: Reflective prompt evolution can\n900\toutperform reinforcement learning. arXiv preprint arXiv:2507.19457, 2025.\n901\t[2] Petar Aleksic, Mohammadreza Ghodsi, Assaf Michaely, Cyril Allauzen, Keith Hall, Brian Roark, David\n902\tRybach, and Pedro Moreno. Bringing contextual information to google speech recognition. In Proc.\n903\tInterspeech, pp. 468–472, 2015.\n904\t[3] Alexei Baevski, Yuhao Zhou, Abdelrahman Mohamed, and Michael Auli. wav2vec 2.0: A framework for\n905\tself-supervised learning of speech representations. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems\n906\t(NeurIPS), 2020.\n907\t[4] Tom B. Brown, Benjamin Mann, Nick Ryder, Melanie Subbiah, Jared Kaplan, Prafulla Dhariwal, Arvind\n908\tNeelakantan, Pranav Shyam, et al. Language models are few-shot learne